window.docContents[251].resolve({"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods--Monotone-Iteration-Between-Barriers":{"contents":"Let the ambient ordered Hilbert space have closed order relation, let the\nlinear form be bounded and coercive, and let  be continuous.\nAssume  and that the semilinear Picard map\nis monotone and preserves .\nIf the iterates from the lower endpoint\nconverge, their limit is the least weak solution in the interval. If the\niterates from the upper endpoint converge, their limit is the greatest weak\nsolution there.Convergence of the iterates is a hypothesis, since order monotonicity gives\nno compactness or strong convergence in the ambient Hilbert space.\n\nContinuity turns each assumed iterative limit into a fixed point. The\norder-interval iteration theorem compares every fixed point with every lower\nor upper iterate, and closedness of the order passes those inequalities to\nthe limit. The fixed-point equation is then rewritten as the semilinear weak\nequation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Semilinear Existence and Stability\u0009Semilinear Elliptic Methods","header":"9.4.1.4. Monotone Iteration Between Barriers","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Semilinear-Existence-and-Stability--Semilinear-Elliptic-Methods--Monotone-Iteration-Between-Barriers"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--The-Expression-Representation":{"contents":"To differentiate a formula, the algorithm needs to see how it is assembled:\nwhich operation is outermost, which subexpressions it acts on, and where\nvariables occur. We store that structure as a finite expression tree, called\nVerified.Expr ι, rather than only its value as a real function. Its\nconstructors include integer and rational constants, variables, the four\narithmetic operations, negation, natural powers, real powers a ^ b with an\narbitrary real exponent (Mathlib's Real.rpow), sin, cos, atan,\nasin, acos, exp, log, and sqrt, together with pi and e. An\nenvironment\n\nassigns a real value to every variable, and Expr.eval ρ e interprets the\nsyntax tree as a real number.\n\nThis is called a deep embedding. A shallow representation by Lean functions\n is convenient for stating analytic theorems, but an\nopaque function does not expose its constituent operations to a symbolic\nalgorithm. The expression tree does: differentiation, normalization, and\ninterval evaluation recurse over those operations. We use it because these\nthree computations can then share one representation. The price is a proof\nthat each computation has the intended meaning when the tree is evaluated.\nThe bridge theorems provide that proof and return statements about ordinary\nreal functions, such as the derivatives used in the earlier PDE chapters.\n\nThe index type carries arity, not a new scalar type. For example,\nVerified.Expr (Fin 2) is a scalar expression in two ordered real\ncoordinates. The expression  therefore denotes a map\n. A vector-valued expression is represented by a finite family of\nscalar expressions, one for each output coordinate.\n\nThe #cas command and theorem tactics recognize supported ordinary Lean\nnotation and build the tree internally; a real power is recognized whether it\nis written x ^ (r : ℝ) or Real.rpow x r, while a literal natural exponent\nkeeps the natural-power constructor.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Proof-producing computation","header":"10.4.2. The Expression Representation","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--The-Expression-Representation"}});